"""

longitudinal_inclusion_rules = """
FIRST-PERSON RULE (applies to every post and every extracted item):
- Extract ONLY the author's OWN GLP-1 treatment experience.
- IGNORE any content where the author is advising, replying to, or describing
  SOMEONE ELSE - e.g. "you should try...", "my wife is on...", "for most people
  nausea fades". Do not create timeline_events, effects, doses, or weights from
  such posts, even when they mention GLP-1 terms.
- A dose, effect, or weight counts ONLY if the author states it about themselves
  (first person: "I", "my", "me"). If it is unclear whose experience it is, do
  NOT extract it.

INCLUSION (whether the journey as a whole is usable):
- include = true  only if, using FIRST-PERSON content only, the journey has:
    * at least 2 of the author's own posts spanning at least 14 days, AND
    * at least one first-person post with treatment-relevant content
      (dose, weight, effect, adherence, or timeline information).
- include = false if the author never describes their own GLP-1 use (e.g. they
  only give advice, ask questions, or discuss others), or the first-person
  content is too sparse to meet the thresholds above.

When include = false:
- set exclusion_reason to a short explanation.
- If excluded because the content is NOT the author's own experience
  (advice-only, about others, questions-only): extract NOTHING - set effects,
  events and metrics to empty. Do not record other people's effects.
- If excluded only because the author's own journey is too short/sparse:
  you MAY still extract their own desired/adverse effects where clearly present.
When include = true: set exclusion_reason = null.
"""

section = """VISUALISATION GROUPING
Every desired effect, adverse effect, confounder, treatment_event, adherence/access
issue and baseline condition must be returned as a SEPARATE item with BOTH:
- specific_label: the exact issue as reported, concise and lower-case. Keep one
  distinct issue per item - never merge two issues (e.g. reduced food noise AND
  reduced cravings) into one item or one label.
- visualisation_group: a broad, chart-ready category, lower-case.

How to choose visualisation_group:
1. PREFER the vocabulary below. If an existing group reasonably fits the concept,
   use it verbatim - even when the wording in the post differs. Match on meaning,
   not exact words.
2. If NO existing group fits, CREATE a new group, following the naming rules below,
   so the same concept gets the same label in any other journey. Do not fall back
   to "other" just to avoid naming something.
3. Use "other" ONLY as a last resort - when no clinically or pharmacovigilance-
   meaningful label can be applied consistently across journeys (e.g. vague,
   off-topic or uninterpretable content). Prefer a real category whenever one exists.

Naming rules for a NEW group (so identical concepts get identical strings across
independent journeys):
- lower-case; a short noun phrase naming the concept, not a sentence.
- keep all specific detail (symptom, dose, number, body part) in specific_label,
  NOT in the group name. The group is the reusable bucket.
- do NOT encode severity, dose, frequency, dates or quantifiers in the group name.
- follow the directional patterns already in use for effects:
  "reduced X" / "improved X" / "increased X" / "worsened X".
- for a medical condition or comorbidity, use the recognised clinical term,
  singular (e.g. "hypothyroidism", "gallbladder disease").
- self-administered supplements -> "supplement use"; prescribed medications ->
  "co-medication". Never combine the two.
- reuse a group you have already created earlier in THIS journey for the same
  concept, so a journey never contains two spellings of one category.

PREFERRED VOCABULARY (by field):
{vocab}"""
